Bennett’s complaint is understandable: he lost approximately $124,000 in foregone gains relative to remaining invested. But suitability and best-interest standards are not judged solely by outcome.

A recommendation can be suitable or in the customer’s best interest even if it performs poorly. Conversely, a profitable recommendation can still be unsuitable.

The relevant question is:

> At the time Pratt recommended the tactical de-risking, did she have a reasonable basis to believe the recommendation was in Bennett’s best interest, considering his investment profile, objectives, risk tolerance, time horizon, liquidity needs, financial circumstances, tax status, and the costs/risks/alternatives?

