That documentation helps her. It shows the recommendation was not arbitrary. It also shows she was trying to manage risk rather than generate commissions.

Also relevant:

- Bennett agreed to the move;
- Bennett was market-aware and financially experienced;
- The assets were moved to low-risk, liquid instruments;
- T-bills yielded approximately 5.3%;
- There were no transaction commissions;
- Pratt did not profit from trading activity.

Those facts reduce the likelihood of a classic suitability violation based on churning, excessive trading, or self-dealing.

But they do not end the analysis. A customer’s consent does not cure an unsuitable or not-best-interest recommendation. Nor does a documented macroeconomic view automatically make a recommendation suitable.

The question remains whether it was reasonable to recommend that this particular customer move such a large portion of an aggressive-growth portfolio to cash.

