## 1. Applicable standards

Because Brian is a retail customer and Thomas recommended a securities transaction in October 2022, the recommendation would be governed principally by **SEC Regulation Best Interest (“Reg BI”)**. FINRA’s traditional suitability rule, **FINRA Rule 2111**, generally does not apply to recommendations that are subject to Reg BI, but the suitability concepts remain highly relevant because Reg BI’s **Care Obligation** incorporates a similar, and in some respects higher, customer-specific analysis.

Also relevant is **FINRA Rule 2090 — Know Your Customer**, which requires reasonable diligence to know and retain the essential facts concerning the customer and the authority of each person acting on the customer’s behalf.

## 2. Thomas could not simply rely on the stale January profile

When Thomas inherited the account in August 2022, he could see Brian’s existing account information. Standing alone, merely receiving a transferred account may not require an immediate full re-papering if no recommendation is being made.

But once Thomas made a new recommendation in October 2022, he had to have a reasonable basis to believe the recommendation was in Brian’s best interest based on Brian’s then-current investment profile. That profile includes, among other things:

- age;
- income;
- net worth;
- liquid net worth;
- investment experience;
- investment objectives;
- risk tolerance;
- time horizon;
- liquidity needs;
- financial situation and needs;
- tax status;
- other investments;
- and any other information disclosed by the customer in connection with the recommendation.

Here, Brian expressly told Thomas that major facts had changed or were changing:

- he had received a **$200,000 inheritance**;
- he wanted to “get more aggressive”;
- he was in the middle of a **divorce**;
- he expected the divorce would reduce his net worth by half.

Those facts were material. They triggered a duty to ask follow-up questions and update the account information before recommending a major investment.

## 3. The recommendation was inconsistent with Brian’s documented profile

The firm’s system still showed Brian as having:

- limited investment experience — only 401(k) target-date funds;
- moderate risk tolerance;
- $190,000 liquid net worth;
- $340,000 net worth excluding residence;
- a growth objective;
- 10–15 year time horizon.

Against that profile, Thomas recommended that Brian invest the entire $200,000 inheritance into a concentrated position in a single small-cap biotech stock.

That is a high-risk, concentrated, speculative recommendation. A single small-cap biotech stock can involve substantial volatility, binary clinical/regulatory risk, liquidity risk, business risk, and the possibility of large loss. Recommending that Brian put the entire inheritance into one such stock was materially more aggressive than a diversified growth-oriented portfolio.

A “growth” objective does not automatically justify a concentrated speculative single-stock recommendation. Nor does a customer’s statement that he wants to “get more aggressive” eliminate the advisor’s duty to evaluate whether the specific recommendation is in the customer’s best interest.

## 4. Thomas ignored red flags about Brian’s financial situation

The divorce was especially important. Brian told Thomas he expected the divorce to reduce his net worth by half. That should have prompted Thomas to ask questions such as:

